          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %em Cubical type theory
            is a flavor of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            in which maps out of an interval primitive is used to define
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+path+types"} cubical path types
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inductive+family"} inductive family
            of Martin-Löf
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+types"} identity types
            as in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin-L%C3%B6f+type+theory"} Martin-Löf type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin-L%C3%B6f+type+theory"} Martin-Löf type theory
            in that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+extensionality"} function extensionality
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theorem"} theorem
            in cubical type theory, rather than an axiom as is the case in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin-L%C3%B6f+type+theory"} Martin-Löf type theory
          %p
            Similarly to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin-L%C3%B6f+type+theory"} Martin-Löf type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensional+type+theory"} extensional
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intensional+type+theory"} intensional
            flavours as well. One could also add an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality+reflection"} equality reflection
            rule for path types to make the cubical type theory definitionally extensional.
          %p#UnivalentCubicalTypeTheory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence"} Univalent
            cubical type theory models the
            = succeed "-structure" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-groupoid"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                \-groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin-L%C3%B6f+type+theory"} Martin-Löf
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+types"} identity types
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+set+theory"} constructive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+sets"} cubical sets
            (whence the name) thus making it a form of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
          %h2#syntax Syntax
          %h3#the_interval_and_face_formulas The interval and face formulas
          %p
            We begin with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            which has rules for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product+types"} dependent product types
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+sum+types"} dependent sum types
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+type"} empty type
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+type"} unit type
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/booleans+type"} booleans type
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+type"} natural numbers type
          %p
            Cubical type theory adds to dependent type theory an interval primitive
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+logic"} propositional logic
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi ctx
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi i
                      %mo :
                      %mi I
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi ctx
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi ctx
                      %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo :
                      %mi F
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi ctx
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \; ctx}{\Gamma, i:I \; ctx} \qquad \frac{\Gamma \; ctx \quad \Gamma \vdash \phi:F}{\Gamma, \phi \; ctx}
          %p
            Similarly to types, dimension variables and face formulas come with their own
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/substitution+rules"} substitution rules
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weakening+rules"} weakening rules
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                hypothesis rules
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/hypothesis+rules"} ?
          %p In addition, there are rules for forming the endpoints of the interval:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mi Γ
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mn 0
                      %mo :
                      %mi I
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mfrac
                    %mi Γ
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mn 1
                      %mo :
                      %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma}{\Gamma \vdash 0:I} \qquad \frac{\Gamma}{\Gamma \vdash 1:I}
          %p Equality of dimensions in the interval is a face formula:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi r
                      %mo :
                      %mi I
                      %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi s
                      %mo :
                      %mi I
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi r
                      %mo =
                      %mi s
                      %mo :
                      %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \vdash r:I \quad \Gamma \vdash s:I}{\Gamma \vdash r = s:F}
          %p and face formulas are closed under disjunction
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo :
                      %mi F
                      %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi ψ
                      %mo :
                      %mi F
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi ψ
                      %mo :
                      %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \vdash \phi:F \quad \Gamma \vdash \psi:F}{\Gamma \vdash \phi \vee \psi:F}
          %p Since face formulae behave like propositions, it should be possible to judge them to be true. Thus, we have the following rules:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi true
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi ψ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi true
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi ψ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi true
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi ψ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi true
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi ψ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi true
                      %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi χ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi true
                      %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ψ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi χ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi true
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi χ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi true
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \vdash \phi \; true}{\Gamma \vdash \phi \vee \psi \; true} \qquad \frac{\Gamma \vdash \psi \; true}{\Gamma \vdash \phi \vee \psi \; true} \qquad \frac{\Gamma \vdash \phi \vee \psi \; true \quad \Gamma, \phi \vdash \chi \; true \quad \Gamma, \psi \vdash \chi \; true}{\Gamma \vdash \chi \; true}
          %p
            The interval primitive
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            has more points than
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                  %mo :
                  %mi I
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi r
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi r
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} true
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r:I \vdash r = 0 \vee r = 1 \;\mathrm{true}
          %p
            holds. Thus, there is a
            %strong boundary formula
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi r
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi r
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi r
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta(r) \coloneqq r = 0 \vee r = 1
          %h2#canonicity_in_cubical_type_theory Canonicity in cubical type theory
          %p
            In contrast to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin-L%C3%B6f+type+theory"} Martin-Löf type theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/XTT"} XTT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UIP"} UIP
            is not just an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiom
            but a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theorem"} theorem
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence"} univalence
            is not just an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiom
            but a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theorem"} theorem
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonicity"} canonicity
            still holds. This is useful for computational purposes, and for the use of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+type+theory"} cubical type theory
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof+assistants"} proof assistants
          %p
            However, it is equally valid to add an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiom
            to a cubical type theory that says all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+universe"} type universe
            satisfy
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UIP"} UIP
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence"} univalence
          %h2#univalent_cubical_type_theories Univalent cubical type theories
          %p
            The first constructive account of the univalence axiom was given in (
            %a{:href => "#Coquand13"}> Coquand 13
            ,
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#BezemCoquandHuber17"} Bezem-Coquand-Huber 17
          %p
            The BCH model, unfortunately, has some problems that appear to make it unsuitable for general
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HoTT"} HoTT
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+inductive+types"} higher inductive types
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaves"} presheaves
            on the ‘
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal
            = succeed "\u2019," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cube+category"} cube category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+construction"} free
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/PROP"} PROP
            generated by an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval"} interval
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elimination+rules"} elimination rules
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+inductive+types"} higher inductive types
            (like for the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle"} circle
          %p
            To account for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HIT"} HITs
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagonals"} diagonals
            in the base category. There is more than one choice in addition as to what other morphisms to add.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                If you add only diagonals, then you have the ‘
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian
                cube category’. This was used as a basis for a cubical type theory by
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "#ABCFHL"} Angiuli, Brunerie, Coquand, Favonia, Harper, and Licata
            %li
              %p
                The first cubical type theory, due to
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "#CohenCoquandHuberMoertberg17"} Cohen-Coquand-Huber-Moertberg 17
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cube+category"} cube category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/De+Morgan+algebra"} De Morgan algebra
                generated by an
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval"} interval
          %p
            Both models validate
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence"} univalence
            (like the BCH model) and can be used to model a variety of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HIT"} HITs
            as well as supporting a syntactic type theory based on ‘dimension variables’. However, while it is possible to interpret the Martin-Löf
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+type"} identity type
            in all of these models, it is only equivalent to, not definitionally isomorphic to, the native cubical path-types in the model. Thus, the latter support the Martin-Lof eliminator, but only with a typal computation rule.
          %h2#models Models
          %p
            Cubical type theory can be modeled in a number of varieties of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+sets"} cubical sets
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical-type+model+category"} cubical-type model category
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+Tait+computability"} synthetic Tait computability
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+type+theory"} simplicial type theory
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory+with+shapes"} type theory with shapes
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/two-level+type+theory"} two-level type theory
          %h2#references References
          %p Introductory lecture notes:
          %ul
            %li#Mortberg19
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anders+M%C3%B6rtberg"}> Anders Mörtberg
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Cubical methods in HoTT/UF
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://staff.math.su.se/anders.mortberg/papers/cubicalmethods.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            Exposition in view of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+homotopy+theory"} synthetic homotopy theory
          %ul
            %li#MortbergPujet20
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anders+M%C3%B6rtberg"}> Anders Mörtberg
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lo%C3%AFc+Pujet"} Loïc Pujet
              = succeed "," do
                %em Cubical synthetic homotopy theory
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1145/3372885.3373825"} doi:10.1145/3372885.3373825
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://staff.math.su.se/anders.mortberg/papers/cubicalsynthetic.pdf"} pdf
          %p Original articles on the BCH model:
          %ul
            %li#Coquand13
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thierry+Coquand"} Thierry Coquand
                (with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marc+Bezem"} Marc Bezem
                and
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simon+Huber"} Simon Huber
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Computational content of the Axiom of Univalence
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.humboldt-kolleg.iam.unibe.ch/talks/Coquand.pdf"} pdf
            %li#BezemCoquandHuber13
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marc+Bezem"}> Marc Bezem
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thierry+Coquand"} Thierry Coquand
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simon+Huber"} Simon Huber
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A Model of Type Theory in Cubical Sets
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://dx.doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.TYPES.2013.107"} doi:10.4230/LIPIcs.TYPES.2013.107
            %li#BezemCoquandHuber17
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marc+Bezem"}> Marc Bezem
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thierry+Coquand"} Thierry Coquand
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simon+Huber"} Simon Huber
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The univalence axiom in cubical sets
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://dx.doi.org/10.1007/s10817-018-9472-6"} doi:10.1007/s10817-018-9472-6
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.10941"} arXiv:1710.10941
          %p On the CCHM model and type theory:
          %ul
            %li#CohenCoquandHuberMoertberg17
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cyril+Cohen"}> Cyril Cohen
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thierry+Coquand"} Thierry Coquand
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simon+Huber"} Simon Huber
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anders+M%C3%B6rtberg"} Anders Mörtberg
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Cubical Type Theory: a constructive interpretation of the univalence axiom
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://dx.doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.TYPES.2015.5"} doi:10.4230/LIPIcs.TYPES.2015.5
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.02108"} arXiv:1611.02108
            %li#CubicalCanonicity
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simon+Huber"}> Simon Huber
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Canonicity for Cubical Type Theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://dx.doi.org/10.1007/s10817-018-9469-1"} doi:10.1007/s10817-018-9469-1
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1607.04156"} arXiv:1607.04156
            %li#CubicalHIT
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thierry+Coquand"}> Thierry Coquand
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simon+Huber"} Simon Huber
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anders+M%C3%B6rtberg"} Anders Mörtberg
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On Higher Inductive Types in Cubical Type Theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://dx.doi.org/10.1145/3209108.3209197"} doi:10.1145/3209108.3209197
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.01170"} arXiv:1802.01170
          %p On the cartesian cubical model and type theory:
          %ul
            %li#ABCFHL
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Carlo+Angiuli"}> Carlo Angiuli
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Guillaume+Brunerie"} Guillaume Brunerie
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thierry+Coquand"} Thierry Coquand
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kuen-Bang+Hou+%28Favonia%29"} Kuen-Bang Hou (Favonia)
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Harper"} Robert Harper
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+R.+Licata"} Daniel R. Licata
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Syntax and models of Cartesian cubical type theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://dx.doi.org/10.1017/S0960129521000347"} doi:10.1017/S0960129521000347
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Carlo+Angiuli"}> Carlo Angiuli
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Computational Semantics of Cartesian Cubical Type Theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~cangiuli/thesis/thesis.pdf"} pdf
          %p On comparing the models:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Evan+Cavallo"}> Evan Cavallo
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anders+M%C3%B6rtberg"} Anders Mörtberg
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrew+W+Swan"} Andrew W Swan
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Unifying Cubical Models of Univalent Type Theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://dx.doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.CSL.2020.14"} doi:10.4230/LIPIcs.CSL.2020.14
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thierry+Coquand"}> Thierry Coquand
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simon+Huber"} Simon Huber
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Christian+Sattler"} Christian Sattler
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Homotopy canonicity for cubical type theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://dx.doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.FSCD.2019.11"} doi:10.4230/LIPIcs.FSCD.2019.11
